 fundamentals point to a Cuellar hold: incumbency, a redrawn district with a friendlier map, and a large cash advantage, while markets and most raters put him clearly ahead. Tijerina is a well-funded, high-profile challenger with national Republican support, so this stays Likely rather than Safe D.", "watch": "A late surge in Republican turnout driven by Trump-aligned campaigning in South Texas, or fresh polling showing the race tightening, would move this toward Lean D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Henry Cuellar (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Henry Cuellar, a longtime incumbent Democrat, seeks reelection in a redrawn South Texas district against Republican Tano Tijerina, a Laredo-area county judge and recent party convert, with a Green Party candidate also on the ballot. The new maps appear to have improved Cuellar's position, and he holds a substantial fundraising edge with more than $1.1 million on hand. Expert raters split between Lean D and Likely D, and prediction markets price his win chances around 85 percent.\n\nTijerina has run an unusually serious campaign for this seat, raising nearly $1.4 million and appearing alongside national Republican figures, including at the RNC midterm convention. Polling is sparse: one August survey showed Cuellar up eleven points, while an older Republican-sponsored poll had him trailing. The weight of evidence favors Cuellar, but the district's Republican lean and the challenger's profile keep the race competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9009, "margin": 7.69}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8852, "margin": 7.0}]}